    "title": "Hypothetical Local Inference: Beyond Tokens Per Second",
    "body": "This post explores a hypothetical scenario involving two local LLM runtimes achieving 18 and 24 tokens per second (tok/s) respectively on the same machine with a single prompt. While tok/s is a common metric, it doesn't tell the whole story. For instance, the hypothetical test did not measure time to first token, which is critical for interactive use cases. Nor was answer quality assessed, leaving open the possibility that the faster runtime might produce less useful output. Furthermore, this limited comparison, based on a single prompt and machine, does not establish a universal winner. Different prompts, hardware configurations, or quantization methods could yield different results. It's important to consider these unmeasured factors when evaluating local inference performance. What other metrics should be considered to get a more complete picture of runtime performance in a local setting?",
