  {
    "title": "Hypothetical LLM Benchmark Limitations: 18 vs 24 tok/s",
    "body": "When considering hypothetical local LLM performance, raw tokens per second (tok/s) can be misleading. In a recent hypothetical test on the same machine using a single prompt, two runtimes yielded 18 tok/s and 24 tok/s. While 24 tok/s appears faster, this comparison is incomplete. Crucially, neither the time it takes for the first token to appear (TTFT) nor the actual quality of the generated answers were evaluated in this hypothetical scenario. A model might generate tokens rapidly but take a long time to start, or produce rapid but nonsensical output. This limited hypothetical test, using only one prompt and one hardware setup, cannot definitively declare one runtime superior. Performance can vary significantly with different prompts, batch sizes, quantization levels, and underlying hardware. It's essential to acknowledge these limitations and avoid drawing broad conclusions from such specific, hypothetical data points. What are your thoughts on interpreting these kinds of hypothetical benchmarks?",
